<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virgil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">70–19 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Vergilius Maro, Vergil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virgil was a Roman poet of the Augustan age, author of the Eclogues, the Georgics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, the epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s legendary Trojan origins. The Aeneid became the national poem of Rome and a school text for centuries, and its lines were quoted on coins and inscriptions. Virgil lived much of his life near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he was buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virgil.jpg" alt="Virgil" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boyd Dwyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgil_mosaic_in_the_Bardo_National_Museum_(Tunis)_(12241228546).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
